# Code Readability and End-of-Task Cleanup Contract

Applies from TASK-001 through TASK-075, including documentation-only tasks. It also defines the default coding-task quality policy Nexees supplies to its agents. A task has multiple small verified changes, not an excuse to modify unrelated files at once.", "Q1 — Code that a human developer can follow
Use descriptive names, explicit control flow, cohesive functions/modules, normal language idioms, consistent formatting and useful types/error handling. Explain the purpose and boundaries of a nontrivial module. Document public interfaces, inputs/outputs, side effects, failure modes and invariants where those are not already clear from the interface. Comments/docstrings must explain what the important code does and why a non-obvious choice exists.

Especially document workspace/device bindings, state transitions, synchronization conflicts, permission decisions, read-only handoff enforcement, archive path checks, retry/idempotency rules and platform restrictions. Keep comments next to the relevant implementation and update them in the same change. Tests should clearly state the behavior and edge case they prove.

Do not narrate every trivial assignment, impose a comment-per-line percentage, hide poor naming behind prose, add repetitive decorative blocks, leave commented-out code, or fabricate authorship. Readability is the goal, not cosmetic imitation of a human author. Preserve upstream notices and generated-file conventions; do not hand-edit generated or vendored code solely to add comments.", "Q2 — Scope and reuse
Read the current file before editing. State its path and purpose of the change. Work on one file and one meaningful change at a time; verify before moving on. Reuse existing helpers and approved components. No speculative abstraction, opportunistic unrelated refactor or second implementation of LCL/import/state functionality. Do not run multiple heavy build/emulator/model processes concurrently on the development laptop without explicit approval.", "Q3 — Mandatory task-close cleanup
Before any task is marked COMPLETE:
1. Inspect the actual diff, files created by this task and pre-existing user changes. Record ownership; unknown/untracked does not mean disposable.
2. Remove only task-owned obsolete scaffolding, superseded code, unused imports/dependencies and temporary experiments/debug output after confirming they are unneeded. Keep required error diagnostics, tests, fixtures, licenses, manuals and audit evidence.
3. Remove task-owned temporary files/staging directories and stop only task-owned test processes. Release locks/handles. Do not delete shared caches or another workspace's artifacts to make the tree appear clean.
4. Review comments/docstrings and names against the final implementation; remove stale explanations. Run formatters/linters on the permitted scope.
5. Update affected manuals, references, task evidence and dependency records. Account for any necessary generated artifacts.
6. Re-run affected compilation, unit/integration/security/regression checks AFTER cleanup. If anything fails, keep the task open, fix within scope, and repeat affected verification. A pre-cleanup green result is insufficient after code or configuration changes.
7. Record the exact final revision/diff, cleanup items, preserved user work, post-cleanup commands/results and residual limitations. A no-op cleanup is valid only with an inspected, documented reason.

No blanket `git clean -fdx`, `git reset --hard`, wildcard deletion, silent stash or automatic deletion of untracked files. No commits, pushes or cleanup beyond the user's approved permissions. A clean/understood diff means all changes are accounted for, not erasing pre-existing work.", "Q4 — Completion state and evidence
Use the existing task/evidence store, not a second database. For coding tasks, completion requires implementation, readability review, cleanup and post-cleanup verification evidence. Diagnostic/status records may be persisted during a read-only orientation, but source changes and cleanup mutations are prohibited until the normal write gate opens.

Documentation-only tasks record appropriate syntax/link/reference checks; unavailable application builds are N/A with a reason, never fabricated PASS. Later implementation tasks still require real executable tests. Runtime project policies may explicitly adjust quality steps, but cannot disable immutable security boundaries or falsely claim verification.", "Q5 — Acceptance
RC-01: nontrivial changed code has accurate purpose/behavior and rationale documentation; trivial boilerplate comments are absent.
RC-02: a task-owned temporary artifact is removed while unrelated untracked user work and necessary tests remain byte-for-byte preserved.
RC-03: a defect introduced by cleanup makes the task remain BLOCKED until post-cleanup tests pass.
RC-04: failed tasks, canceled work and read-only orientation cannot bypass permission gates through a cleanup operation.
RC-05: every closure records readability, cleanup, manual impact and final-revision test evidence; a missing record blocks progression.", "C1 — Workspace Contract
Every workspace has:
- stable workspace ID;
- project root;
- workspace type;
- workspace-specific UI state;
- file-tree state;
- agent bindings;
- task/project state;
- security scope.", "C2 — Foreground Workspace Contract
The foreground workspace controls what the user sees. It does not implicitly control which workspace an agent may modify.", "C3 — Agent Binding Contract
Each running agent is bound to:
- one explicit workspace ID;
- one project root/security scope;
- one task/project state;
- one provider/model at a time.
A model change does not change the agent's workspace binding.", "C4 — File Tree Contract
The left file tree reflects the current foreground workspace:
- coding workspace -> coding project tree;
- LCL workspace -> LCL project tree.
Background agent activity must never force a tree switch.", "C5 — Persistent State Contract
Project continuity must survive:
- provider/model switch;
- UI workspace switch;
- app restart;
- recoverable crash;
- Android/Desktop reconnect.", "C6 — LCL Authority Contract
When LCL is enabled, validated LCL rules/contracts/tasks/read-order/stop-conditions/verification become authoritative project constraints according to the configured LCL mode.", "C7 — LCL Workspace Contract
Clicking the LCL integration opens an LCL workspace inside Nexees. Nexees remains the workspace shell.", "C8 — Provider Contract
