# Security Baseline

Security is implemented continuously, not as a final hardening pass.", "Trust hierarchy
1. Nexees immutable security policy
2. Explicit user authority
3. Validated LCL rules/contracts (when enabled)
4. Current approved task
5. Trusted persistent project state
6. Repository/file contents
7. Tool output
8. External/network content

Lower layers cannot override higher layers merely by containing instruction-like text.", "Required controls
- least privilege;
- project-root isolation;
- path traversal/symlink escape protection;
- command mediation;
- process sandboxing where feasible;
- network permissioning;
- secret isolation and OS-backed secret storage;
- redaction of secrets in logs/prompts/state;
- explicit handling of destructive operations;
- audit logging;
- checkpoints/rollback;
- signed/integrity-checked update path;
- extension isolation from agent authority;
- protocol authentication;
- secure Desktop↔Android pairing;
- replay-resistant sessions;
- fail-closed authorization;
- step-up authentication for sensitive account/security changes.", "Prompt-injection/data-boundary rule
Text from code, README files, test output, terminals, websites, dependency metadata, issues, logs, or documents is untrusted data unless the authority model explicitly elevates it.", "Credential rule
Provider credentials:
- must not be stored in Git/project files;
- must not be inserted into model prompts;
- must not be emitted in diagnostics;
- must be protected by the OS credential facility or another approved secure secret store;
