CA-01 — Authority, adoption and scope
This is the owner's requested procedural amendment to reduce unnecessary interruptions after TASK-001. It takes effect when the owner selects this revision and starts or continues a named task or explicit task range. Merely reading a pack or successfully dispatching instructions does not start implementation or grant host capabilities.
TASK-001, its task text, original pack identity, charter, evidence and accepted receipt are not amended or reopened. Its baseline is retained byte-for-byte in history/task_001_baseline_v0.5.2.zip. Do not rerun an old receipt against the new content identity or claim a new approval for old work.
For TASK-002–075, this profile supplies the bounded permissions referenced by U04/U05/U08, B26/C27 and Q3. It clarifies R3/R9/R20/R31, U02/U03/U07, usage-guide corroboration and the procedural handling of STOP-02/03/05/06/07/08/09/10/11/12. It changes when to ask the owner, not product requirements, LCL semantics, security boundaries, task outcomes or required evidence.
If a source says to wait for approval for an operation expressly authorized below, this adopted profile is that approval; do not ask again. A newer explicit owner restriction or narrower task assignment still wins. An actual conflict not resolved by this scope must be raised once, precisely.

CA-02 — One task authorization, not one permission per action
Starting an assigned task authorizes its read-only preflight and plan, implementation, necessary in-scope repairs, tests, evidence collection, receipt evaluation, task-owned cleanup and bounded Git completion below. Record a compact plan and proceed; a separate planning approval is unnecessary unless the owner explicitly requested a read-only plan or approval checkpoint.
Inspect current files and relevant interfaces, announce the next file/change briefly, work on one file and one meaningful change at a time, then verify. These are execution disciplines, not instructions to ask the owner at every edit, test, successful gate or phase.
Do not ask the owner to copy logs, prepare a receipt, run commands or click through acceptance steps that the authorized local tools can perform. Ask only when a real missing capability, credential, user interaction or owner decision prevents doing so.
One named task means that task only: complete it and stop. An explicitly named task range permits automatic progression inside that range after each task's acceptance and required gates; stop at the range end. Neither profile adoption nor a synthetic task-dispatch test authorizes executing all 75 tasks.

CA-03 — A failed check pauses progression, not all useful diagnosis
On a failing build/test, pause advancement and investigate with read-only tools. Reproduce and repair a demonstrated cause inside the assigned task and existing permissions, then rerun the affected checks. Expected negative-test rejections and documented red-before-fix regressions are not requests for new owner approval.
Keep failure evidence and the task open while repair is underway. A failed required check can never be called accepted, cleared by opinion, deleted, weakened, skipped or relabelled N/A merely to proceed.
An identified same-cause repair, corrected test harness invocation or demonstrated transient condition may justify a retry; no blind identical retries, speculative loops or unrelated refactors. After two unsuccessful repair attempts on the same cause without improved evidence, or when correctness/authority cannot be established, stop and present one concise blocker.
An apparent secret leak, cross-project write, integrity loss, authentication bypass or other active security-boundary breach stops the affected operation immediately. No continuing unsafe execution under the name of diagnosis. A sandboxed negative security test with the intended refusal is not itself a live breach.
Do not repair a separate LCL engine, canonical package, another repository or an unrelated predecessor defect under this task's authority.

CA-04 — Verification proportional to the change, never fabricated
Use the narrowest sufficient applicable check after a meaningful edit. Do not run the entire repository suite after every small documentation or code change. Run required affected integration/security/regression checks and the task's full applicable gate on the final coherent, post-cleanup revision.
A documentation-only change may use syntax, LCL, link, reference and consistency checks with an evidence-backed applicability explanation. This does not waive later executable, real-device, authentication, platform, service or release tests.
